> > (though there is always a trap door on the island to facilitate
> > escape).
> 
> This seems too generous to me. If the player is silly enough to get
> themselves marooned it should be left up to them to get themselves off
> it. If all else fails they can always swim.

If all the entrances into an island room are closed doors then the
player cannot get out unless he can open or break one from afar, which
requires special equipment.

And it's not always up to the player if he gets marooned or not. 
Teleportation can strand the player, as well as falling onto the
island from the level above.  And there's a chance that the moat will
be too thick to cross by swimming on the sides of the room with the
critical door.

Also, deeper in the dungeon there's a chance the island will have a
lava moat instead of water, which is not trivial to cross.


> How about rivers of lava (or glaciers) in very deep levels (maybe
> Gehennom?) Or a possibility of a bridge (as in draw-) across it?

Drawbridges are potent sources of arbitrary death.  Also, rivers are
several spaces wide, and it's difficult to make sure the drawbridge
would appear in a logical place.  I'll think about it and see what I
can come up with.

Gehennom levels are generated in a completely different way than
"normal" levels, and I'd probably have to figure out some of the
details of the special level code to mess around with the mazes.


> I notice from glancing at your patch that you disallow rivers and islands
> on the same level - not very logical! Ideally the island should occur in
> the middle of the river...

Well, there are plenty of illogical rooms in Nethack, heh.  The reason
island rooms and rivers don't appear on the same level is actually to
avoid the case where an island room is in the middle of the river,
creating the possibility of a non-passable level if that room is part
of the critical route between staircases.  (If there's no river, then
there's always a pick-axe from the Mines in the worst case scenario.)

Note that despite all my efforts, there's still a chance that the
player will get trapped by an island: if the player teleports or falls
into a level into a room that is blocked from the critical route by an
island, and has no means of getting past it, then he is truly stuck. 
Tests indicate that this is, in fact, more common than you might
think.  I'm still trying to think of a solution to that one.

